    15	**ABSTRACT**
    16	
    17	
    18	During test execution, automated software tests can interfere, i.e.,
    19	their results can deviate depending on their (possibly interleaved)
    20	execution order. Such interference imposes severe restrictions on
    21	regression testing, when execution order is not or cannot be controlled for, as they can lead to non-deterministic deviations of test
    22	results giving false indication of regressions in the code base. While
    23	the phenomenon has been extensively studied for Java and Python
    24	projects, it remains unclear if or how the obtained results apply
    25	for other languages with different testing practices. Our study contributes to filling that gap by reporting results from a large-scale
    26	study on test interference in 134 C projects.
    27	To cope with the combinatorial explosion of test execution counts
    28	when testing with all possible test orders, we propose and evaluate four novel dynamic reduction strategies for test permutations,
    29	which yield massive reductions in the number of test sequences to
    30	execute. As these strategies are specific to the resources that tests
    31	interfere on, rather than the language in which the code is written,
    32	we expect them to be useful for the study of test interference in
    33	other languages beyond C.
    34	Based on the results obtained with these reductions, our results
    35	indicate that test order dependencies are far less common in C
    36	projects, compared to Java or Python, and that other aspects (concurrency, CPU time) more frequently threaten test result stability.
    37	
    38	
    39	**KEYWORDS**
    40	
    41	
    42	Test interference, flaky tests, C
